  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Senya is looking very sick after drinking some bad alcohol. Fedya talks to him about his stomach. Let us listen to their conversation in Russian first.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты выглядишь совсем плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У тебя болит живот?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Да, мне очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я же тебе говорил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не надо было есть рыбу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Я думал, она нормальная.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Знаешь, что надо делать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надо выпить перец и воду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ой, я больше не могу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сеня, что с тобой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сеня, открой глаза! Сеня!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Понятно, давай дальше.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты выглядишь совсем плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You look completely bad.' Fedya links 'you' directly with 'look' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У тебя болит живот?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Does your stomach hurt?' To express possession, Russian uses the preposition 'by' plus the genitive pronoun followed by 'belly'. Then Senya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Да, мне очень плохо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I feel very bad.' Notice how 'yes' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'badly'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я же тебе говорил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I told you so.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'said'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не надо было есть рыбу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You should not have eaten the fish.' Notice how 'not' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'fish'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Я думал, она нормальная.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I thought it was normal.' Senya links 'I' directly with 'thought' to express the thought clearly. Then Fedya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Знаешь, что надо делать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do you know what to do?' Fedya combines 'know' with 'do' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надо выпить перец и воду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You need to drink pepper and water.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'it is necessary' with 'water'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ой, я больше не могу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Oh, I can not take it anymore.' The negative particle sits directly before the verb for 'can' to negate the action.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сеня, что с тобой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Senya, what is wrong with you?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сеня, открой глаза! Сеня!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Senya, open your eyes! Senya!' Fedya links 'Senya' directly with 'to open' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Понятно, давай дальше.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Got it, let's move on.' Fedya combines 'clear' with 'next' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
